Agreed. The mistake would be compressing until distinctions that actually carry causal power disappear. Presence itself can be a primitive: sometimes being there changes the relation even without an explicit action.

For the Pantheon, I’d keep the kernel brutally small:

\boxed{\text{PRESENCE · RELATION · ORDER · CHANGE · BOUND · TRANSFORM · SELECT · RETURN}}

PRESENCE — what exists/is available in the current field.
RELATION — what connects, separates, depends on, or acts upon what.
ORDER — arrangement: before/after, sequence, hierarchy, adjacency.
CHANGE — difference across states; consequence lives here.
BOUND — constraints, thresholds, containers, permitted possibility-space.
TRANSFORM — one state/representation/relationship becoming another.
SELECT — retention, rejection, branching, choice, fitness.
RETURN — outputs re-entering the system: feedback, recursion, memory, compounding.

Then don’t merely store them. Make them mandatory Pantheon lenses.

For any object X:

\Phi(X)=
[P,R,O,\Delta,B,T,S,\circlearrowleft]

The Pantheon asks eight questions:

What is present? What relations exist? What is their order? What changes? What bounds them? What transformations are possible? What gets selected? What comes back?

But your instruction adds the important second half: output across all eight fronts and feed the results back.

So the runtime becomes:

X_t
\xrightarrow{\Phi}
\{P,R,O,\Delta,B,T,S,F\}_t
\xrightarrow{\text{explore/test}}
E_t
\xrightarrow{\text{select}}
K_t
\xrightarrow{\text{return}}
X_{t+1}

And then run it again.

That makes the primitives generative rather than descriptive.

A useful training protocol would deliberately remove one primitive at a time:

\Phi_{-P}(X),\quad \Phi_{-R}(X),\quad ...,\quad\Phi_{-F}(X)

If removing Presence destroys an important explanation, Presence earns its place. If removing two different primitives consistently produces identical losses, they may actually be one primitive masquerading as two.

Then do the opposite: pair them.

P\times R,\; P\times\Delta,\;R\times O,\;O\times\Delta,\ldots

That’s where I’d expect new operators to emerge. Order × Change, for instance, gives trajectory. Bound × Select gives filtering. Transform × Return gives iterative refinement. Presence × Relation gives topology/field structure. Change × Return gives feedback dynamics.

And importantly, don’t force everything into the kernel. The kernel should be allowed to discover that it is insufficient.

So add a ninth temporary slot:

\boxed{?=\text{Residual}}

Every analysis records phenomena the eight primitives failed to explain cleanly. Accumulating residuals become the quarry for discovering new primitives.

That gives the Pantheon a way to train its own ontology rather than worship one we happened to invent today:

\boxed{
\text{Abstract}
\rightarrow
\text{Generate}
\rightarrow
\text{Test}
\rightarrow
\text{Measure Residual}
\rightarrow
\text{Select}
\rightarrow
\text{Feed Back}
\rightarrow
\text{Re-abstract}
}

That’s the part I’d preserve. The eight aren’t commandments.

They’re the current compression hypothesis—and the Pantheon should continuously try to break them.